"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[10079],{410079:e=>{e.exports="# How to update YAML (partial merge)\n\nPartial YAML is merged at the spec root; the merge is not simple concatenation or text patch—it follows fixed semantics below.\n\n---\n\n## Structure: Full Path from Root\n\n**Every key in your partial must be written with all upstream nodes from the document root.**\n\n- Partial YAML is merged at the root. If you omit a parent key, the merge does not know where to attach the nested key.\n- **Correct:** Start at the root and include every parent along the path to the key you are setting (e.g. `spec` → `silver` → `transform` → `presetOverrides` → `modifyTables`).\n- **Incorrect:** Starting at an intermediate node (e.g. only `silver:` at top level when the spec root has `metadata`, `spec`, `schedule`). That would create or overwrite a top-level `silver` key instead of updating `spec.silver`.\n\n**Rule:** Always nest under the actual root keys of the spec. If the spec has `metadata`, `spec`, and `schedule` at the top level, your partial must use those same root keys (e.g. `spec:` then `silver:` under it), not a fragment that assumes a different root.\n\n---\n\n## Merge Semantics\n\n### Objects (nested maps)\n\n- **Deep merge:** The partial is merged into the target at each key.\n- **Effect:** Keys present only in the target are kept. Keys present in the partial **add** or **overwrite** at that path. Sibling keys are preserved.\n- **Implication:** You can send only the paths you want to change; you do not need to repeat the entire spec.\n\n### Arrays (lists)\n\n- **Override, not append:** If the partial includes an array for a key, that array **replaces** the entire array in the target for that key.\n- **Effect:** The target array is not concatenated with the partial array. The partial array is the full new value for that key.\n- **Implication:** To change an array, your partial must contain the **complete** new array (all items that should exist after the update), not just new or changed items.\n\n---\n\n## Rules for Generating Partial YAML\n\n0. **Include all upstream nodes from the root**  \n   Write every path from the document root. For example, to set `spec.silver.transform.presetOverrides.modifyTables`, your partial must have `spec:` at the top level, then `silver:` under it, then `transform:`, then `presetOverrides:`, then `modifyTables:`. Do not emit a fragment that starts at `silver:` or `modifyTables:` at the root.\n\n1. **Updating or adding a single field (scalar or nested object)**  \n   Include the **full path from root** to that field and the new value. Other keys at the same or other levels remain unchanged.\n\n2. **Changing an array**  \n   Include the key and the **full** array you want after the update. Every item that should remain or be added must appear in this array. Do not send only the new element expecting it to be appended.\n\n3. **Adding a new key (including a new array)**  \n   Include the **full path from root** to the new key and its value (or full array). It will be added; existing keys are unchanged.\n\n4. **Removing a key or array item**  \n   For arrays: send the full new array **without** the item(s) to remove. For top-level or object keys: removal is not supported by merge semantics; the partial cannot delete a key (only add/overwrite).\n\n5. **Do not assume**\n   - Array concatenation (partial array + target array)\n   - Merge-by-index (first item with first item)\n   - Merge-by-key (e.g. by `id`) — arrays are replaced as a whole.\n\n---\n\n## Examples\n\n### Example 1: Update a single scalar (schedule)\n\n**Target spec (excerpt):**\n\n```yaml\nschedule:\n  atLeastEvery: '1h'\n  enabled: true\nmetadata:\n  name: my-rule\n  version: 1\n```\n\n**Goal:** Change schedule to run every 15 minutes.\n\n**Correct partial:**\n\n```yaml\nschedule:\n  atLeastEvery: '15m'\n```\n\n**Result after merge:** `schedule.atLeastEvery` is `\"15m\"`, `schedule.enabled` stays `true`, `metadata` unchanged.\n\n**Incorrect:** Sending the full spec again when only one field changed is unnecessary (but still correct). Prefer minimal partials.\n\n**Also incorrect:** Omitting the root. If the real spec has a top-level `spec:` and you only need to change schedule, your partial must still nest under the root, e.g. the partial above uses `schedule:` at top level only when the spec root is the rule/spec itself (so `schedule` is a root key). If the document root were `{ spec: { schedule: ... } }`, then the partial would need to be `spec:\\n  schedule:\\n    atLeastEvery: \"15m\"`.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: Add a new nested key\n\n**Target has:** `spec.source`, `spec.sourceType`, no `spec.silver` yet.\n\n**Goal:** Add silver table transform configuration.\n\n**Correct partial:**\n\n```yaml\nspec:\n  silver:\n    transform:\n      presetOverrides:\n        modifyTables:\n          - name: my_silver\n            addFields:\n              - name: is_admin\n                expr: \"role LIKE '%admin%'\"\n```\n\n**Result after merge:** `spec.source` and `spec.sourceType` unchanged; `spec.silver` added.\n\n**Why correct:** The path starts at root with `spec:`, then `silver`, `transform`, etc. All upstream nodes from the root are included.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2b: Wrong — missing upstream from root\n\n**Target spec root** has top-level keys: `metadata`, `spec`, `schedule`. You want to add silver config under `spec`.\n\n**Incorrect partial (missing root):**\n\n```yaml\nsilver:\n  transform:\n    presetOverrides:\n      modifyTables:\n        - name: my_silver\n```\n\n**Why incorrect:** This puts `silver` at the **root** of the document. After merge, the spec would have a new top-level key `silver` instead of `spec.silver`. The existing `spec` object would be unchanged, and `spec.silver` would not exist.\n\n**Correct partial:** Use the actual root key `spec`, then nest `silver` under it (as in Example 2).\n\n---\n\n### Example 3: Replace an array (critical)\n\n**Target spec (excerpt):**\n\n```yaml\nsilver:\n  transform:\n    presetOverrides:\n      modifyTables:\n        - name: table_a\n          addFields: []\n        - name: table_b\n          addFields:\n            - name: x\n              expr: '1'\n```\n\n**Goal:** Add a new field to the **first** table (`table_a`) and keep `table_b` as-is.\n\n**Correct partial:** You must send the **full** new `modifyTables` array with both tables and the new field on `table_a`:\n\n```yaml\nsilver:\n  transform:\n    presetOverrides:\n      modifyTables:\n        - name: table_a\n          addFields:\n            - name: new_field\n              expr: '2'\n        - name: table_b\n          addFields:\n            - name: x\n              expr: '1'\n```\n\n**Incorrect partial (wrong):**\n\n```yaml\nsilver:\n  transform:\n    presetOverrides:\n      modifyTables:\n        - name: table_a\n          addFields:\n            - name: new_field\n              expr: '2'\n```\n\n**Why incorrect:** The merge **replaces** the whole `modifyTables` array. So with this partial, `table_b` would disappear. Always send the complete array you want.\n\n---\n\n### Example 4: Add one item to an array\n\n**Target:**\n\n```yaml\nitems:\n  - id: 1\n    name: first\n  - id: 2\n    name: second\n```\n\n**Goal:** Add a third item `{ id: 3, name: third }`.\n\n**Correct partial:** The full new array (all three items):\n\n```yaml\nitems:\n  - id: 1\n    name: first\n  - id: 2\n    name: second\n  - id: 3\n    name: third\n```\n\n**Incorrect partial:**\n\n```yaml\nitems:\n  - id: 3\n    name: third\n```\n\n**Why incorrect:** That would **replace** `items` with a single-element array; the first two items would be lost.\n\n---\n\n### Example 5: Remove an item from an array\n\n**Target:** `items: [{ id: 1, name: a }, { id: 2, name: b }, { id: 3, name: c }]`  \n**Goal:** Remove the item with `id: 2`.\n\n**Correct partial:** The full array without that item:\n\n```yaml\nitems:\n  - id: 1\n    name: a\n  - id: 3\n    name: c\n```\n\nThere is no “delete one element” operation; you supply the entire new array.\n\n---\n\n## Checklist Before Emitting Partial YAML\n\n- [ ] **Root path:** Does my partial include **all upstream nodes from the root** for every key I set? (No starting at an intermediate node like `silver:` at top level when the spec root has `spec:`.)\n- [ ] **Arrays:** Did I include the **full** array for every key I’m changing? (No “append only” or “one new element”.)\n- [ ] **Paths:** Are the keys and nesting correct for the spec schema (e.g. `spec.silver.transform.presetOverrides.modifyTables`)?\n- [ ] **Minimal:** For scalar or object updates, did I send only the paths I intend to change?\n- [ ] **No assumptions:** I did not assume concatenation, merge-by-index, or merge-by-key for arrays.\n\n---\n\n## After Calling the Update Tool: Verify the Merged Result\n\nWhen `pageUpdateLakewatchDatasourceSpec` or `pageUpdateLakewatchDetectionRuleSpec` succeeds, the tool response includes `data` — the full merged YAML that was applied to the spec. **Always check this merged YAML** to confirm the intended changes are present before reporting success to the user.\n\n### What to check\n\n1. **The changed key/value is present** — The field you updated appears with the new value.\n2. **Unchanged keys are preserved** — Keys you did not intend to modify still exist with their original values.\n3. **Arrays are correct** — For any array you replaced, the full new array is present (no dropped items, no unintended extras).\n4. **No unintended overwrites** — No top-level key was accidentally created instead of a nested path (e.g. `silver` at root instead of `spec.silver`).\n\n### If the merged YAML does not match your intent\n\n- The partial YAML you sent was likely structured incorrectly (wrong root path, wrong array handling).\n- Do **not** call the update tool again with the same partial — diagnose the mismatch first.\n- Re-read the merge semantics above, correct your partial, and call the tool again with the fixed partial.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n| Intent            | What to put in partial YAML                                                              |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| (Always)          | **Full path from root** — include every parent key from document root to the key you set |\n| Change one field  | Full path from root + new value                                                          |\n| Add a new key     | Full path from root to the new key + its value (or full array)                           |\n| Change an array   | Full path from root + the **entire** new array (all items)                               |\n| Add to an array   | Full path from root + the full new array = existing + new item(s)                        |\n| Remove from array | Full path from root + the full new array minus removed item(s)                           |\n\n**Remember:** (1) Always include all upstream nodes from the root. (2) For any key that is an array, the partial’s array **overrides** the target’s array; there is no append or index-based merge.\n"}}]);
